<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</h1><p class="st-meta">1385 CE – 1569 CE · capital Krakow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Jagiellonian Poland, which with Lithuania defeated the Teutonic Knights, built a powerful noble democracy and enjoyed a Renaissance golden age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1385 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a> arranged the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, grand duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, who was baptised and crowned in 1386 as Wladyslaw II Jagiello. His dynasty linked the Polish Crown to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and for a time also ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Together, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Grunwald</a> in 1410, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466 brought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia</a> and the port of Gdansk under the Crown. The nobility won growing privileges, and the 1505 statute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihil-novi">Nihil novi</a> required the consent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> for new laws. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> centre, home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Copernicus</a> and to Italian builders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> castle.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>, last of the male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellons</a>, had no heir. To preserve the union, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 merged the kingdom with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, with a common elected king and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)" width="320" height="270"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderT86 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Reconstruction_of_the_Banner_of_Sigmund_III_Vasa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1385 CE – 1569 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Krakow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wladyslaw II Jagiello, Casimir IV, Sigismund I the Old</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crown of the Kingdom of Poland, Jagiellonian Poland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1477&amp;focus=kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569" aria-label="Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 692L5457 693M5457 692L5646 778M